# WHICH SHIPPED FILES EACH BROWSER SUITE READS — the one declared half of `proof-for.sh`.
#
# ========================================================================================
# WHY THIS IS DECLARED AND NOT DERIVED, WHICH IS A MEASUREMENT AND NOT A PREFERENCE
# ========================================================================================
# Everywhere else in `app/ui/tests/` an inventory is read off disk and a typed list is the
# defect. The three derived rules in `proof-for.sh` follow that: a touched suite is itself,
# a touched library is whoever requires it, a touched reference shot is whoever names its
# directory. This file is the fourth rule, and it is declared because the derivation was
# tried on this tree and measured:
#
#   * a plain basename grep over the 55 suites gives `main.js` 33 suites and `index.html`
#     37 — near enough the whole set, which is not a map, it is a full run with extra steps;
#   * with comments stripped — leaving only the lines that are code — it gives `home.js`
#     ZERO. Every mention of `home.js` inside `home.js`'s own suite is prose in its header.
#
# Both numbers are correct and both are useless, for the same reason: a browser suite does
# not NAME the source it exercises. It opens `index.html` and drives the rendered surface.
# The relation between a shipped file and the suite that would catch a change in it is
# behavioral, and behavior is not greppable.
#
# `lib/ui-sources.js` does not close the gap either, though the name suggests it: it is the
# manifest of what SHIPS, derived from `index.html` and reconciled against the directory.
# It answers "which files are product" and never "which suite reads which".
#
# ========================================================================================
# WHAT STOPS IT ROTTING
# ========================================================================================
# The same thing that stops `ui-sources.js`'s ROLES table rotting: it is RECONCILED IN BOTH
# DIRECTIONS, by `proof-for.sh`, on every single invocation, and a mismatch is exit 2 rather
# than a quiet wrong answer.
#
#   * a suite in `ui/tests/` with no row here          -> refuses, naming the suite
#   * a row naming a suite that does not exist         -> refuses, naming the row
#   * a row naming a path that is not in the tree      -> refuses, naming both
#   * two rows for one suite                           -> refuses
#
# So this file cannot be short and it cannot be stale. It can still be WRONG — a row can
# name too little — and the honest guard against that is the direction it errs in: a row is
# a SUPERSET, exactly as `run-tests: inputs` is. Naming a file the suite barely touches
# costs one suite run. Failing to name one costs a defect.
#
# WHERE THIS BELONGS EVENTUALLY. Each row wants to live in its own suite, as a
# `// proof-for: inputs …` header, for the reason `run-tests.sh` gives for reading its
# declaration out of the suite: the suite is the only thing that knows what it reads, and a
# second place to edit is the defect. It is here instead because `ui/tests/` had another
# owner on the day this landed. Moving a row into its suite and deleting it here is a
# one-line change per suite and needs no change to `proof-for.sh` beyond reading the header
# first.
#
# FORMAT
#   <suite.js>  <path> [<path>…]      paths are repository-relative
#   @name       <path> [<path>…]      an alias, usable in any row
#   @allui                            NOT typed: computed by proof-for.sh as every shipped
#                                     file under app/ui/ (tests/ and node_modules/ aside).
#                                     The eight suites that `require("./lib/ui-sources")`
#                                     genuinely read all of them.
#   <dir>/      a DIRECTORY INPUT (the trailing slash is the marker): the suite reads the
#               whole tree under it, including files that do not exist yet. Any changed path
#               under it SELECTS the suite; it COVERS nothing, so it can never make a file
#               under it look proven (the same rule as a script suite's `run-tests: inputs`).
#               For a suite that checks a claim about a whole tree, such as docs-claims.js's
#               test counts, whose inputs are every Rust file in every crate. That suite
#               also checks its own row against what it read (its last check), so a new claim
#               reading a new place cannot land with nothing to select it.
# ========================================================================================

@shell   richos/app/ui/index.html richos/app/ui/main.js richos/app/ui/style.css richos/app/ui/attachments.js richos/app/ui/output-panel.js
@home    richos/app/ui/home.js richos/app/ui/home.css richos/app/ui/home/field-engine.js richos/app/ui/home/field-prep.js richos/app/ui/home/field-ref.js
@splash  richos/app/ui/splash.js richos/app/ui/splash-library.js richos/app/ui/splash.css richos/app/ui/theme-boot.js
@phone   richos/app/ui/phone.js richos/app/ui/qr.js

# FIXTURES (`ui/tests/fixtures/*`) are named on the row of the suite that LOADS them, found by
# reference in the suite's code (`path.join(__dirname, "fixtures", "<name>")`,
# `readFixture("<name>")`). The two on-demand preloads no suite loads are named on the rows of
# the suites their own usage lines run them with (`node --require ./fixtures/<preload>.js
# <suite>.js`, in the preload's header and ui/tests/README.md). A fixture on no row is
# UNCOVERED, and the land check refuses it until a row names it (2026-09-29, the automatic
# land check, esc-20260929T082408Z-c96cefea).

# ---- the eight that read the whole shipped manifest ------------------------------------
# Each of these requires `./lib/ui-sources` and walks every file it classifies: the state
# inventory, the contrast sweep and the dialect pass are whole-surface gates by definition.
affordances.js          @allui richos/app/src-tauri/src/ richos/app/crates/
appearance.js           @allui
contrast.js             @allui richos/app/ui/tests/contrast-debt.json
corrections.js          @allui richos/app/ui/tests/fixtures/loro-proposal.json richos/app/ui/tests/fixtures/loro-proposal-asked-again.json richos/app/ui/tests/fixtures/heard-candidate.json
feedback.js             @allui richos/app/ui/tests/fixtures/feedback-vocabulary.json richos/app/ui/tests/fixtures/feedback-previews.json richos/app/ui/tests/fixtures/feedback-entries.json
memory.js               @allui
setup.js                @allui
splash.js               @allui

